data.content:
Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` with `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>`. Both compile and run without issues:

```csharp
Span<nint>  nints  = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> nuints = stackalloc nuint[3];
```

This works because `nint`/`nuint` (native-sized integers, introduced in C# 9) are unmanaged types, which is the requirement for `stackalloc`. On this 64-bit system, `nint.MaxValue` is `9223372036854775807` (same as `long.MaxValue`), confirming they're 8 bytes wide here.
